  "questions": [
    "Are chords in C major all rooted on C?",
    "Why is there a diminished row without a detail button?",
    "Can a minor key use a major V?"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "heading": "Read the table by scale degree",
      "body": "Each row stacks alternate notes from the selected scale. In major, the resulting qualities are major, minor, minor, major, major, minor and diminished. Uppercase Roman numerals indicate major triads; lowercase indicate minor, with ° for diminished.",
      "source_ids": [
        "F-OMT",
        "F-MAJOR"
      ],
      "evidence_status": "[已核实] source formulas; [推断] original voicings/editorial examples"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Keep minor forms explicit",
      "body": "The D minor table starts with natural minor: D E F G A B-flat C. It therefore includes A minor. The separate raised-leading-tone options use C-sharp, producing A major and C-sharp diminished; do not silently replace the natural-minor row.",
      "source_ids": [
        "F-MINOR",
        "F-OMT"
      ],
      "evidence_status": "[已核实] source formulas; [推断] original voicings/editorial examples"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Try a key-specific result",
      "body": "In E major, I–IV–V is E–A–B. Read the complete notes in each row before playing: E–G-sharp–B, A–C-sharp–E, then B–D-sharp–F-sharp. In F major, remember B-flat in the scale and its derived chords.",
      "source_ids": [
        "F-MAJOR"
      ],
